uilder context and lack of systemic symptoms moderately favors neurovascular compression over plexopathy (LR+=6.0 vs LR-=0.2)."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's bodybuilder status and lack of systemic symptoms make neurovascular compression more plausible than brachial plexopathy or thoracic tumor?", "summary": "Bodybuilder status increases likelihood of mechanical neurovascular compression (LR+=4.7) while decreasing plexopathy (LR-=0.2). This moderately favors compression over alternative explanations."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the subacute progression (weeks) and presence of systemic symptoms (weight loss, fever) be explained by a non-neoplastic process such as steroid-induced myopathy or chronic brachial plexopathy?", "summary": "Subacute progression and systemic symptoms slightly favor neurovascular compression (LR+=2.3) but remain neutral for plexopathy and strongly reduce confidence in thoracic neoplasm due to diabetes (LR+=0.07)."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever) and hypertension more consistent with a systemic malignancy (B1) than a localized brachial plexopathy?", "summary": "Hypertension weakly against thoracic neoplasm (LR+=0.33) while weight loss strongly excludes it (LR+=0.07). These findings contradict the systemic malignancy hypothesis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever) inconsistent with a benign mechanical compression syndrome and instead indicative of a systemic process?", "summary": "Weight loss and fever moderately against benign mechanical compression (LR-=0.2) but don't directly support systemic malignancy. This weakens B3's plausibility."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.291], ["B1", 0.263], ["B2", 0.243]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers detected", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch posterior (0.263) < 0.75. Dangerous alternative B5 (posterior 0.121, danger 0.7) remains unresolved. Tree depth (2) < max depth (3). Estimated_remaining_value (0.82) suggests actionable discriminators remain."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.82, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2631578947368421, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Apical lung tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Brachial plexopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Cerebral infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Scalenus anticus syndrome"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Subclavian steal syndrome"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Constitutional symptom'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.291}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Thoracic Neoplasm with Neurological Involvement", "posterior": 0.667, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B2", "B5"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=both]\n\nBrachial Plexopathy vs Neurovascular Compression Syndrome:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nBrachial Plexopathy vs Cerebrovascular Disease with Subacute Presentation:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: balance problems, delirium, dizziness, double vision, fainting\n\nNeurovascular Compression Syndrome vs Cerebrovascular Disease with Subacute Presentation:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: balance problems, delirium, dizziness, double vision, fainting\n\nRelated to Neurovascular Compression Syndrome: carpal tunnel syndrome, meralgia paresthetica, peripheral neuropathy\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Neurovascular Compression Syndrome"}
